11f1-a9b9-434ea329edcd/file-6y3zli1mpb677rh.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p><b>Understanding the role of LiBH<sub>4</sub></b></p>\n<p>LiBH<sub>4</sub> is a reducing agent. It converts <b>esters</b> into <b>alcohols</b>, but it does not reduce the <b>carboxylic acid group</b>. This selectivity helps in predicting which groups will change during the reaction and which will remain the same.</p>\n<p><b>Forming the products</b></p>\n<p>In the reaction, only the ester group gets reduced. The carboxylic acid group stays unchanged. Based on this, different compounds form as products, which are labelled as <b>P, Q, R,</b> and <b>S</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Relationship between products</b></p>\n<p>The pairs <b>P and Q</b> have the same connectivity but differ in the spatial arrangement of atoms. Similarly, <b>R and S</b> also differ in spatial arrangement. These are examples of <b>diastereomers</b>, which are a type of stereoisomer that are not mirror images of each other.</p>\n<p><b>Type of isomerism</b></p>\n<p>Since the difference arises due to the geometry around the double bond or restricted rotation, <b>P and Q</b> as well as <b>R and S</b> are specifically <b>geometrical isomers</b>, which are a form of diastereomers.</p>\n<p>Therefore, <b>P and Q are diastereomers</b> and <b>R and S are diastereomers</b> because they are <b>geometrical isomers</b>.</p>"}]}